  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct human absolute oral bioavailability values for steroid-like molecules are consistently below 20%: β-sitosterol 0.41% (C01, C14, C20), testosterone 3.66% (C09), testosterone undecanoate 6.8% (C11, C24), danazol 11–19% in the fasted state (C04, C07, C12, C25), abiraterone acetate <10% fasted (C10, C16, C23), and oleanolic acid shows poor human exposure (C02, C19). This establishes a transferable low-F pattern for the query's steroid class.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C04","C07","C09","C10","C11","C12","C14","C16","C19","C20","C23","C24","C25"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Phenolic steroids undergo extensive first-pass conjugation and efflux: fulvestrant's poor oral bioavailability is attributed to UGT/SULT inactivation in the GI tract and liver (C36), estradiol undergoes intestinal Phase II metabolism to glucuronide/sulfate conjugates (C71, C79), and estriol is a P-gp substrate with an efflux ratio of ~3.2 that collapses in the presence of a P-gp inhibitor (C77, C78). The query contains a phenol and a 1,2-diol, matching this presystemic-loss liability.",
      "card_ids": ["C36","C71","C77","C78","C79"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance and metabolic-stability records show that steroid analogs, especially estradiol, undergo P450-mediated oxidative metabolism (C87, C89, C90) and glucuronidation (C85, C88), with measurable intrinsic/hepatic clearances for testosterone and epitestosterone (C80, C81, C84, C86), CYP-mediated metabolism of 2MeO-E2 (C82), and estrone hydroxylation (C91). This supports hepatic first-pass loss for the query's steroid scaffold, though none is a direct query measurement.",
      "card_ids": ["C80","C81","C82","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "High-permeability records for hydrocortisone, buprenorphine, and samidorphan (C68, C70, C72, C73, C74, C76) are not query-specific and do not contradict a low F prediction, because high intestinal permeability can coexist with extensive presystemic metabolism. Ethinyl estradiol being a substrate for P-gp, BCRP, CYP3A, and UGT (C69) is mechanism-only evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C68","C69","C70","C72","C73","C74","C76"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Conflicting high-F descriptions for stigmasterol (C30) and DMAU (C40) are not transferable to the query or are contradicted by measured low-F data for the same compound (C06, C38), so they do not support a high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C30","C40","C06","C38"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C80","C81","C82","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No query-specific human PK, AUC, Cmax, dose, or formulation data are available.",
    "L6 hepatic clearance and metabolic-stability records are for other steroid analogs (estradiol, estrone, testosterone, epitestosterone, 2MeO-E2, ent-estradiol), not the query.",
    "First-pass conjugation and hepatic metabolism for the query are inferred from structural similarity and analog mechanism, not measured directly.",
    "Query-specific solubility, permeability, efflux, and hepatic clearance are not experimentally measured.",
    "The exact query structure is not matched to a training context with a measured F value."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains low and is strengthened by L6. Direct human absolute F values for steroid-like molecules are consistently below 20%, and the query's phenol plus 1,2-diol matches the well-documented presystemic conjugation/efflux liability seen for fulvestrant, estradiol, and estriol. L6 hepatic clearance and metabolic-stability records add mechanistic support for hepatic first-pass loss in steroid analogs, but are not direct query measurements; no new evidence overturns the low prediction."
}

